gm-method (occlusion self-occlusion fix class transfers imperfectly across tiers): when a proven self-occlusion mitigation (elevation lift, margin, hysteresis, motion-aware verdict expiry) fixes one occlusion-query tier (e.g. terrain tiles), applying the SAME mitigation to a structurally-similar but separate tier (e.g. a generic reused entity-occlusion library serving vegetation/rocks) is a reasonable first move but must be independently live-verified, not assumed to transfer -- the underlying box geometry, query timing, and candidate density can differ enough that the fix's effect is unconfirmed even when code-correct by static inspection. Also: a long-lived browser/Chromium test session degrades under sustained heavy use (30+ consecutive verb dispatches) -- WebGL context issues, a stuck DOM loading curtain that never clears despite the game genuinely ticking underneath, and eventual spool-watcher instability requiring a hard reboot. When this happens mid-investigation, the honest move is to flag the harness degradation explicitly (not claim a fix works or fails based on data gathered while the harness was unhealthy) and defer final verification to a fresh session, rather than forcing more dispatches against a demonstrably degraded environment.